# Project-generated artifacts — large, regenerable
data/raw/
data/processed/
# Episode trajectories live under data/dataset_<seed>/runs/ and are NOT committed
# (covered by `runs/` + `*.npz` below). The dataset's metadata.json is committed
# on purpose — it fully describes the dataset and can regenerate it:
#   python scripts/generate_dataset.py --from-metadata data/dataset_<seed>/metadata.json
# Throwaway sweep / probe corpora from dev investigations. The `runs/` + `*.npz`
# rules already keep the bulk out, but each dir's metadata.json is not covered and
# would ride along on a careless `git add`. Named datasets (data/dataset_<n>/) keep
# their committed metadata.json on purpose — see the note above.
# No trailing slash on `data/_*`: probes also drop a stray console log *beside* the
# directory (data/_probe2_log.txt), which a directory-only rule left exposed.
data/_*
data/*_sweep/
data/*_sweep[0-9]/
data/*_probe/
data/dagger_*/

runs/
checkpoints/
logs/
# Console captures from ad-hoc probe runs. Nothing tracked is a .log, and a probe's
# terminal output is never the artifact — the dataset's metadata.json is.
*.log
outputs/
wandb/
mlruns/
*.npz
*.parquet
*.pt
*.pth
*.ckpt

# ...except every checkpoint behind a documented number. Training runs land in the
# ignored outputs/policy/runs/; docs/results/checkpoints/ is the durable copy, so a
# reader can *run* a policy rather than only read about one. Small enough to commit
# (largest single file ~5 MB). See docs/results/checkpoints/README.md.
!docs/results/checkpoints/
!docs/results/checkpoints/**

# ...and the recorded human corpus, for the opposite reason to every other corpus
# in data/. Generated datasets commit only their metadata.json because the
# trajectories rebuild from a seed on demand (dataset_vision/ and
# dataset_official_ft/ have no episodes on disk at all). data/recorded/ is 54 live
# teleoperation episodes from 2026-07-03: no seed reproduces them, so the files are
# the artifact rather than a cache of it, and `runs/` + `*.npz` above would drop the
# only human data in the project. Trajectories only (52 MB) -- the wrist frames under
# imgs/ stay ignored (85 MB across 12,432 files) and are re-rendered from the replayed
# episode by scripts/dev/render_trajectory.py. The directories are re-included
# explicitly because git cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded.
# See docs/results/../data/recorded/metadata.json and the corpus-staging notes.
!data/recorded/runs/
!data/recorded/runs/*/
!data/recorded/runs/*/episode.npz
# Re-excluded *after* the directory re-includes above: nothing else covers the wrist
# frames (they are .jpg, and the `runs/` rule stopped applying once the directories
# were re-included), so without this the 12,432 of them ride in on the exception.
data/recorded/runs/*/imgs/

# ...and the 32 blinded human-operator trials, for the same reason: a person
# drove them, so no seed rebuilds them, and they are the input the demo footage renders
# from. Same three-line shape as above -- git cannot re-include a file whose parent
# directory is excluded. No re-exclude is needed here (unlike the wrist frames above):
# scripts/dev/stage_blind_trial_corpus.py copies *only* episode.npz out of
# runs/blind_trial/, leaving the per-trial console.log behind, so the destination
# directories hold nothing else to sweep in. See data/blind_trial/metadata.json and
# docs/results/human-trial-result.md.
!data/blind_trial/runs/
!data/blind_trial/runs/*/
!data/blind_trial/runs/*/episode.npz

# Media: ignore generated captures (outputs/, runs/) but ALLOW the committed
# submission media. Without the exceptions below the comment was aspirational —
# `assets/media/demo.mp4` was silently ignored by the *.mp4 rule.
*.mp4
*.avi
*.mov
!assets/media/
!assets/media/**

